<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/evolution-natural-selection">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Evolution by natural selection is biology's unifying theory — the frame that makes every
    other fact in the subject make sense. Darwin published it in 1859 after two decades of
    caution; the mechanism is four plain steps and one deep consequence:
    <strong>populations change over generations because the environment filters
    reproduction</strong>. It explains both the diversity of life and the perfect fit of
    organisms to their niches — without any foresight at all.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four steps — simple enough for a poster, deep enough for a century of research" widget="equationStep" config={evoCfg} />

  <h2>Evidence that built the consensus</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fossils in sequence:</strong> whale ancestors with legs, feathered dinosaurs, Tiktaalik's fish-to-limb transition — predicted by the tree, found where the tree said to dig.</li>
    <li><strong>Living evidence:</strong> antibiotic resistance evolving in real time (bacteria are evolution sped up); Darwin's finches' beaks shifting measurably after droughts (Grant decades study); pesticide-resistant insects within seasons.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared quirks:</strong> the same bones in whale flippers, bat wings and human hands; broken vitamin-C genes shared with other primates. Inherited from common ancestors — design wouldn't include the leftovers.</li>
    <li><strong>Molecular clocks:</strong> DNA divergence rates match fossil dates independently — two methods, one answer.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1859); Grant & Grant, How and Why Species Multiply; Coyne, Why Evolution Is True.</p>
</TopicPage>
